1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,719
The pharmacy kiosk on Floor 2 is showing
English labels to a Spanish-speaking
patient again. Ops restarts the process.

2
00:00:06,839 --> 00:00:12,359
The drug catalog still points at the
staging URL because someone exported the
wrong shell variables on the thin client.

3
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:25,480
Meanwhile the waiting-room board never
lights up when a prescription is ready —
the notifier writes a log line and hopes a
polling job notices. Those three failures
are not BeanFactory failures. The services
are wired.

4
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:38,079
What is missing is the application layer
Spring stacks on top of the factory:
messages, environment, events, and
resource loading. ApplicationContext
extends BeanFactory. You still getBean.

5
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:53,880
You also get MessageSource for i18n,
Environment / PropertyResolver for
profiles and properties, an event bus via
ApplicationEventPublisher, and
ResourceLoader / ResourcePatternResolver
for classpath and file resources.

6
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:09,359
Most real applications program to an
ApplicationContext —
AnnotationConfigApplicationContext,
ClassPathXmlApplicationContext, or Boot's
context — not to the raw factory API. Look
at this java for a moment.

7
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:13,480
Walk the "ready" press on the kiosk after
a successful context refresh.

8
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:23,240
Refresh has already built singletons: the
ResourceBundleMessageSource is registered
under the well-known MessageSource role,
PrescriptionReadyListener is a bean (and,

9
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:34,240
if annotated with @EventListener or
implementing ApplicationListener,
subscribed through the multicaster), and
FulfillmentService holds three
collaborators that are themselves context
services.

10
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:39,320
markReady calls
env.getRequiredProperty("pharmacy.catalog-
url").

11
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:51,760
Environment resolution walks registered
PropertySource instances — system
properties, OS environment variables,
application.yml, profile-specific files —
in documented precedence order.

12
00:01:51,880 --> 00:02:09,879
Staging URL on the thin client means a
wrong source won, not a missing bean.
Next, messages.getMessage("rx.ready", …,
locale) loads i18n/kiosk_es.properties (or
falls back through the basename chain).

13
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:20,560
Missing key → NoSuchMessageException
unless you configured a default message.
Then events.publishEvent(...) hands the
event to ApplicationEventMulticaster.

14
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:27,520
By default that delivery is synchronous on
the caller's thread: the listener updates
WaitingRoomBoard before markReady returns.

15
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:38,120
Same bean graph a BeanFactory would build
— plus these services participating as
first-class collaborators. Failure mode
symptoms map cleanly onto the three Floor
2 incidents.

16
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:52,080
English-only UI with a Spanish Locale:
missing i18n/kiosk_es.properties on the
classpath, wrong basename (i18n/kiosk vs
messages/kiosk),

17
00:02:52,200 --> 00:03:01,760
or Locale never set on the request —
MessageSource falls back to the default
bundle and operators blame "i18n is
broken" when the file was never packaged.

18
00:03:01,880 --> 00:03:15,360
Staging catalog URL after restart:
pharmacy.catalog-url resolved from an
exported shell variable that outranked the
intended ConfigMap/file source;
env.getProperty returns the wrong string
without throwing,

19
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:19,000
while getRequiredProperty only helps when
the key is absent.

20
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:29,920
Waiting-room board silent: listener not
registered (component scan missed the
package), event type mismatch
(PrescriptionReadyEvent vs a similarly
named DTO),

21
00:03:30,039 --> 00:03:34,639
or someone assumed publishEvent was async
and looked for a queue that does not
exist.

22
00:03:34,759 --> 00:03:40,479
Process kill mid-handler loses the in-
memory event — it is not Kafka. Trade-
offs.

23
00:03:40,599 --> 00:03:53,280
ApplicationContext buys a standard refresh
lifecycle, i18n, Environment post-
processors, resource patterns, and an
event multicaster at the cost of heavier
startup than a bare factory.

24
00:03:53,400 --> 00:04:03,159
For the pharmacy kiosk that cost is the
product: you need those services. For the
warehouse CLI next door, a
DefaultListableBeanFactory may still be
enough.

25
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:13,280
Boot's SpringApplication always builds an
ApplicationContext; you rarely construct
one by hand in production apps, but
understanding what it adds explains why
MessageSource, Environment,

26
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:17,639
and ApplicationEventPublisher inject
without you writing factory lookups.

27
00:04:17,759 --> 00:04:25,959
Misconception unique to
ApplicationContext: "Publishing an event
means asynchronous, reliable messaging."
Inside a single context,

28
00:04:26,079 --> 00:04:33,599
publishEvent is typically synchronous on
the caller's thread unless you configure
an ApplicationEventMulticaster with an
async executor.

29
00:04:33,719 --> 00:04:46,839
Exceptions in a listener propagate to the
publisher by default. Losing the process
mid-handler loses the event. Another
misconception: "ApplicationContext
replaces BeanFactory." It extends it.

30
00:04:46,959 --> 00:04:52,639
getBean still bottoms out in the factory;
the context wraps richer services around
the same core.

31
00:04:52,759 --> 00:05:00,560
The kiosk finally speaks the right
language when Locale and bundles line up,
and the board lights when the listener is
actually subscribed.

32
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:13,400
That success raises a sharper question
about the metadata itself: when the plugin
team wants to load payment adapters from
YAML at startup, what exactly is Spring
storing for each bean before any instance
exists?
